.TH NAT-GATE 1 "September 2026" "nat-gate 2.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nat-gate \- manage iptables port forwarding through Tailscale tunnels
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nat-gate
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nat-gate
is a CLI tool for managing iptables NAT rules to forward incoming traffic
to machines on your Tailscale network. It simplifies exposing services running
behind NAT through a gateway server with a public IP.
.PP
All rules created by nat-gate are tagged with identifiable comments for safe
management and removal.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
Preview changes without executing iptables commands. Shows what would be done
without actually modifying any rules.
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Output results in JSON format for scripting and automation.
.TP
.BR \-\-backend " " \fIname\fR
Netfilter backend: \fIiptables\fR (default) or \fInftables\fR. Can also be
set via the \fBNAT_GATE_BACKEND\fR environment variable.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help information.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Initialize the system for port forwarding. Enables IP forwarding and checks
that required dependencies (iptables) are installed.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Also enable IPv6 forwarding.
.RE
.TP
.B add \fIprotocol\fR \fIport\fR \fItarget\fR
Add a port forwarding rule.
.RS
.TP
\fIprotocol\fR
The protocol to forward: \fBtcp\fR or \fBudp\fR.
.TP
\fIport\fR
Port number or range to forward (e.g., 443 or 8000-8080).
.TP
\fItarget\fR
Tailscale IP address to forward traffic to.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " " \fIiface\fR
Only forward traffic arriving on the specified interface.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Use ip6tables for IPv6 forwarding.
.RE
.TP
.B del \fIprotocol\fR \fIport\fR
Delete a port forwarding rule.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Delete from ip6tables instead of iptables.
.RE
.TP
.B list
List all active nat-gate forwarding rules.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Show IPv6 rules instead of IPv4.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Show system status including IP forwarding state, iptables installation,
active rule counts, and network interfaces.
.TP
.B doctor
Diagnose system-level forwarding problems in one pass: backend tooling,
kernel IP forwarding, the Tailscale interface, loaded rules, systemd
unit/backend consistency, boot persistence, and conntrack availability.
Each finding includes the command that fixes it. Read-only; exits
non-zero when any check fails. \fB--json\fR emits the full check list
for scripting.
.TP
.B metrics
Serve Prometheus metrics over HTTP (default port 9110) for scraping:
per-rule packet and byte counters, live session count, and build info.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-port " " \fIport\fR
HTTP listen port (default 9110).
.TP
.BR \-\-once
Render the exposition text to stdout once and exit.
.RE
.TP
.B backup [\fIfile\fR]
Export all nat-gate rules to a JSON backup file.
.RS
.TP
\fIfile\fR
Output file path (default: ./nat-gate-backup.json). Use \fB-\fR for stdout.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Export IPv6 rules instead of IPv4.
.RE
.TP
.B restore \fIfile\fR
Restore nat-gate rules from a JSON backup file.
.RS
.TP
\fIfile\fR
Backup file to restore from.
.RE
.TP
.B apply
Apply rules from a YAML configuration file.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIfile\fR
Path to config file. If not specified, searches for:
.br
  ~/.config/nat-gate/rules.yaml
.br
  /etc/nat-gate/rules.yaml
.TP
.BR \-\-check
Compare the config against live rules and report drift without changing
anything. Exits non-zero when they diverge.
.RE
.TP
.B sessions
Show currently active forwarded sessions with the real client IP addresses
(requires conntrack-tools).
.TP
.B log \fIsubcommand\fR
Manage connection logging. Player IP addresses are visible only on the
gateway; the logger captures them from the kernel conntrack table.
.RS
.TP
.B show
Query logged connection events.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-since " " \fIduration\fR
Only events newer than this (e.g., 30m, 24h, 7d).
.TP
.BR \-\-client " " \fIip\fR
Filter by client IP address.
.TP
.BR \-\-port " " \fIport\fR
Filter by forwarded port.
.TP
.BR \-\-rule " " \fImarker\fR
Filter by rule marker (e.g., nat-gate:tcp:25565).
.TP
.BR \-\-event " " \fItype\fR
Filter by event type: new or end.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit " " \fIn\fR
Show at most the n most recent records.
.TP
.BR \-\-dir " " \fIdir\fR
Log directory (default /var/lib/nat-gate).
.RE
.TP
.B top
Show top clients by traffic volume from completed sessions.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-since " " \fIduration\fR
Only aggregate events newer than this.
.TP
.BR \-\-clients " " \fIn\fR
Number of clients to show (default 10).
.RE
.TP
.B rollup
Daily per-rule traffic summaries from completed sessions.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-days " " \fIn\fR
Number of days to summarize (default 7).
.TP
.BR \-\-dir " " \fIdir\fR
Log directory (default /var/lib/nat-gate).
.RE
.TP
.B daemon
Run the logging daemon in the foreground (used by the systemd service
nat-gate-logger.service; requires CAP_NET_ADMIN and conntrack-tools).
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-dir " " \fIdir\fR
Directory for the JSONL log (default /var/lib/nat-gate).
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-bytes " " \fIn\fR
Rotate after this many bytes (default 10485760).
.TP
.BR \-\-keep " " \fIn\fR
Number of rotated files to keep (default 5).
.RE
.TP
.B status
Show logging configuration and on-disk state.
.RE
.TP
.B service \fIinstall\fR|\fIuninstall\fR|\fIstatus\fR
Manage the nat-gate systemd service that applies rules on boot.
.RS
.TP
.BR install " " \-\-with\-logging
Also install and enable the connection-logging daemon
(nat-gate-logger.service).
.TP
.BR install " " \-\-with\-metrics
Also install and enable the Prometheus metrics exporter
(nat-gate-metrics.service, port 9110).
.PP
The installed units record the active backend (from the global
\-\-backend flag or NAT_GATE_BACKEND) as an Environment= line in
nat-gate.service and nat-gate-logger.service, so boot-time apply and
the logger target the same backend you installed with. Re-run install
after switching backends.
.TP
.BR install " " \-\-with\-proxy
Also install and enable the PROXY-protocol daemon
(nat-gate-proxy.service).
.RE
.TP
.B proxy \fIsubcommand\fR
Manage PROXY-protocol forwarding. A userspace TCP proxy listens on the
gateway and injects a PROXY protocol header (v1 or v2) so the upstream
server (Velocity, Paper, HAProxy-adjacent) sees the real client IP instead
of the gateway's NAT address. TCP only; UDP is not supported.
.RS
.TP
.B add \fItcp\fR \fIport\fR \fItarget\fR [\fItarget\-port\fR] \-\-proxy\-protocol \fIv1|v2|none\fR
Add a PROXY forwarding rule. The protocol must be \fBtcp\fR. The
target port defaults to the listen port. Refuses to collide with an
existing iptables DNAT rule (delete it first with \fBnat-gate del\fR).
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-proxy\-protocol " " \fIv1|v2|none\fR
PROXY protocol version to inject (default v2).
.RE
.TP
.B del \fIport\fR
Delete a PROXY forwarding rule by listen port.
.TP
.B list
List all PROXY forwarding rules.
.TP
.B daemon " " [\-\-dir " "\fIdir\fR]
Run the proxy daemon in the foreground (used by nat-gate-proxy.service).
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-dir " " \fIdir\fR
Override the log directory (default /var/lib/nat-gate).
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B tailscale
List available Tailscale peers and their IP addresses for quick reference.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
nat-gate can read rules from a YAML configuration file. The default locations are:
.PP
.RS
~/.config/nat-gate/rules.yaml
.br
/etc/nat-gate/rules.yaml
.RE
.PP
Example configuration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rules:
  - protocol: tcp
    port: 443
    target: 100.64.0.5
  - protocol: tcp
    port: 8000-8080
    target: 100.64.0.5
    interface: eth0
  - protocol: udp
    port: 51820
    target: 100.64.0.10
    ipv6: false
  - protocol: tcp
    port: 25565
    target: 100.64.0.20
    limit: 30/min
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Initialize the system:
.B sudo nat-gate init
.TP
Add a HTTPS forwarding rule:
.B sudo nat-gate add tcp 443 100.64.0.5
.TP
Add a rule for a specific interface:
.B sudo nat-gate add tcp 80 100.64.0.5 -i eth0
.TP
Add a port range:
.B sudo nat-gate add tcp 8000-8080 100.64.0.5
.TP
Preview a rule addition:
.B sudo nat-gate --dry-run add tcp 443 100.64.0.5
.TP
List rules in JSON format:
.B sudo nat-gate --json list
.TP
Backup rules to a file:
.B sudo nat-gate backup my-rules.json
.TP
Restore rules with preview:
.B nat-gate --dry-run restore my-rules.json
.TP
Apply rules from config:
.B sudo nat-gate apply
.TP
List Tailscale peers:
.B nat-gate tailscale
.SH TUI
.TP
.B tui
Launch the interactive terminal interface. Lists rules with live traffic
statistics and a live sessions panel.
.RS
.TP
.B s
Toggle the live sessions panel (active forwarded flows with real client
IPs; refreshes every 5 seconds, requires root).
.TP
.B a
Add a rule (Enter on the target field opens the Tailscale peer picker).
.TP
.B d
Delete the selected rule (asks for confirmation).
.TP
.B f
Flush all rules (asks for confirmation).
.TP
.B 6
Toggle IPv4/IPv6 mode.
.TP
.B ?", " h
Show keybinding help.
.RE
.SH HOW IT WORKS
nat-gate manages two types of iptables NAT rules for each forwarding entry:
.PP
.B PREROUTING (DNAT)
\- Rewrites the destination IP address of incoming packets to the target
Tailscale IP.
.PP
.B POSTROUTING (MASQUERADE)
\- Ensures return traffic is properly routed back through the gateway.
.PP
All rules are tagged with a comment in the format \fBnat-gate:<proto>:<port>\fR
for safe identification and removal.
.PP
With \fB\-\-backend nftables\fR the same rule pairs live in a dedicated
\fInat-gate\fR nftables table per address family instead of the iptables
nat chains; deletion uses nftables rule handles. All listing, logging,
and backup commands work identically with either backend.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/nat-gate/rules.yaml
User configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/nat-gate/rules.yaml
System-wide configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/nat-gate/nftables.conf
Saved nftables rules (include it from /etc/nftables.conf to restore at
boot when using \fB\-\-backend nftables\fR).
.TP
.I /etc/sysctl.d/99-nat-gate.conf
IP forwarding configuration created by nat-gate init.
.TP
.I /var/lib/nat-gate/connections.jsonl
Connection log written by the logging daemon (rotated .1 ... .5).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/nat-gate.service
.br
.I /etc/systemd/system/nat-gate-logger.service
.br
.I /etc/systemd/system/nat-gate-proxy.service
systemd units installed by \fBnat-gate service install\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred. Error message is printed to stderr (or as JSON if --json flag is used).
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Linux with iptables
.IP \(bu 2
Root/sudo access for modifying iptables rules
.IP \(bu 2
Tailscale installed and connected
.IP \(bu 2
conntrack-tools for \fBsessions\fR and connection logging
.IP \(bu 2
iptables-persistent recommended for rule persistence
